$O$ is the centre. Points $C$ and $D$ are on the same arc $AB$.
[tikz]
% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[],"reference":"circle-angle-terminology theory.workedExamples[0].question_text; source-adapted geometry; ordinary outlines and labels black; no fill"}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\draw[thick] (O) circle (1.6);
\fill (O) circle (1.5pt) node[below right] {$O$};
\coordinate (A) at (215:1.6);
\node[below left] at (A) {$A$};
\coordinate (B) at (325:1.6);
\node[below right] at (B) {$B$};
\coordinate (C) at (55:1.6);
\node[above right] at (C) {$C$};
\coordinate (D) at (125:1.6);
\node[above left] at (D) {$D$};
\draw (A)--(B);
\draw (O)--(A);
\draw (O)--(B);
\draw (C)--(A);
\draw (C)--(B);
\draw (D)--(A);
\draw (D)--(B);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(a) Name the angle at the centre subtended by $AB$.
(b) Name the two angles at the circumference subtended by $AB$.
(c) Name the chord that subtends $\angle ACB$.
(d) Why is $\angle CAB$ not the angle subtended by $AB$ at $C$?
(e) What does the word subtends mean here?

$O$ is the centre. Points $R$ and $S$ lie on opposite arcs $PQ$.
[tikz]
% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[],"reference":"circle-angle-terminology theory.workedExamples[1].question_text; source-adapted geometry; ordinary outlines and labels black; no fill"}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\draw[thick] (O) circle (1.6);
\fill (O) circle (1.5pt) node[below right] {$O$};
\coordinate (P) at (145:1.6);
\node[above left] at (P) {$P$};
\coordinate (Q) at (35:1.6);
\node[above right] at (Q) {$Q$};
\coordinate (R) at (90:1.6);
\node[above] at (R) {$R$};
\coordinate (S) at (270:1.6);
\node[below] at (S) {$S$};
\draw (P)--(Q);
\draw (R)--(P);
\draw (R)--(Q);
\draw (S)--(P);
\draw (S)--(Q);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(a) Does $\angle PRQ$ stand on the major or minor arc $PQ$?
(b) Does $\angle PSQ$ stand on the major or minor arc $PQ$?
(c) Do these two angles stand on the same arc? Explain.

$O$ is the centre. Chord $KL$ divides the circle into a major segment and a minor segment.
[tikz]
% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[],"reference":"circle-angle-terminology worked example; adapted from Foundation Q4 and Development Q9; ordinary outlines and labels black; no fill"}
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (K) at (150:1.6);
\coordinate (L) at (30:1.6);
\coordinate (M) at (260:1.6);
\coordinate (N) at (85:1.6);
\draw[black,thick] (O) circle (1.6);
\fill[black] (O) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below right] at (O) {$O$};
\node[above left] at (K) {$K$};
\node[above right] at (L) {$L$};
\node[below] at (M) {$M$};
\node[above] at (N) {$N$};
\draw[black] (K)--(L);
\draw[black] (K)--(M)--(L);
\draw[black] (K)--(N)--(L);
\draw[black] (K)--(O)--(L);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(a) Name the angle subtended by $KL$ at the circumference in the major segment.
(b) Name the angle subtended by $KL$ at the circumference in the minor segment.
(c) Name the angle subtended by $KL$ at the centre.
(d) Name the chord that subtends $\angle KOL$.
